\title{Exact Bond Dimension of Periodic Combs, and the Limits of\\
Tensor-Train De-Quantization of Order Finding}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 13, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
De-quantization --- the classical emulation of a quantum algorithm under the
structural hypotheses the quantum algorithm implicitly requires --- has
repeatedly dissolved claimed exponential speedups in linear algebra and machine
learning. The common mechanism is low rank. This paper asks whether the same
mechanism applies to the central state of quantum order finding, the periodic
comb $\sum_{x<n,\,x\equiv x_0\,(r)}|x\rangle$, and answers with an exact
formula. Reshaping the amplitude vector across a bipartite cut $n=PQ$, we prove
that the Schmidt rank --- equivalently the minimal matrix-product-state bond
dimension --- is exactly $\min\!\big(P,\,r/\gcd(r,Q)\big)$ for $0<r\le Q$,
independent of the offset $x_0$. On a binary register with $r=2^tm$, $m$ odd,
this becomes the \emph{odd-part law} $\min(2^a,m)$: the power-of-two part of the
period is free, the odd part is rigid. The comb is a product state across a
binary cut if and only if its period is a power of two, and at a balanced cut a
comb of period $2^a-1$ on $L=2a$ qubits forces bond dimension $\ge 2^{L/2}-1$.
In the divisible case $n=mr$ we also compute the bond dimension of the
Fourier-transformed comb, $\min(P,m/\gcd(m,Q))$, and deduce a complementarity
theorem: the product of the bond dimensions before and after the transform is at
most $n/Q$, so at a balanced cut one side is always at most $n^{1/4}$.
Tensor-train emulation of the Fourier transform is therefore provably efficient
exactly when $r\mid n$ --- a hypothesis a binary register with odd order can
never satisfy. The de-quantization boundary is located precisely at a
divisibility relation, and quantum order finding lies on the hard side of it.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} de-quantization, matrix product states, tensor
trains, bond dimension, Schmidt rank, Dirac comb, quantum Fourier transform,
order finding.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{De-quantization and the low-rank hypothesis}

A recurring pattern in quantum algorithmics is this: a quantum algorithm is
advertised as exponentially faster than any classical competitor; closer
inspection reveals that its input model presupposes strong structure --- most
often low rank or low effective dimension --- and a classical randomized
algorithm exploiting the same structure achieves comparable performance, killing
the exponential separation. Three templates are typically named:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Bounded-entanglement simulation.} An $n$-qubit state with
entanglement entropy at most $S_{\max}$ across every cut admits a
matrix-product-state representation of bond dimension $D=O(e^{S_{\max}})$,
reducing storage from $O(2^n)$ to $O(nD^2d)$.
\item \textbf{Low-rank matrix inversion.} A symmetric positive-definite matrix of
numerical rank $\rho\ll N$ can be inverted classically by randomized sketching in
time polynomial in $\rho$ and logarithmic in $N$, bypassing quantum phase
estimation.
\item \textbf{Tensor-train Fourier emulation.} If a state admits a low-rank
tensor-train representation, the quantum Fourier transform can be applied
classically by alternating contractions of core tensors with local phase
operators in $O(nD^2)$ operations, with singular-value truncation between sweeps.
\end{enumerate}

Template 3 is the one that matters for cryptography, since the quantum Fourier
transform is the single component of order finding with no obvious classical
analogue: it aggregates $\Theta(n)$ amplitudes coherently in
$\mathrm{polylog}(n)$ gates. The question this paper settles is whether the
state on which order finding applies that transform admits a low-rank
tensor-train representation. It does not, and we compute exactly how badly it
fails.

\subsection{Contributions}

An exact rank formula for the reshaped periodic comb across an arbitrary
bipartite cut (Theorem~\ref{thm:main}), with matching explicit constructions on
both sides; an operational if-and-only-if characterisation of the minimal bond
dimension (Theorem~\ref{thm:oper}); the odd-part law for binary registers and its
product-state dichotomy (Theorems~\ref{thm:oddpart}--\ref{thm:dichotomy}) with an
exponential lower bound at the balanced cut (Theorem~\ref{thm:exponential}); a
support-only lower bound insensitive to the actual amplitude values
(Theorem~\ref{thm:support}); the transform law and complementarity theorem in the
divisible case (Theorems~\ref{thm:transform} and~\ref{thm:compl}); and the
localisation of the de-quantization boundary at the divisibility $r\mid n$,
together with two precise conjectures extending exact-rank hardness to
approximate-rank hardness.

\section{Setting and definitions}

Throughout, $n$ is the dimension of a quantum register (for qubits $n=2^L$), and
states are unnormalised vectors in $\mathbb{C}^n$; normalisation never affects
rank.

\begin{definition}[Bipartite cut and reshape]
A \emph{cut} is a factorisation $n=PQ$. Every index $x\in\{0,\dots,n-1\}$
decomposes uniquely as $x=pQ+q$ with $0\le p<P$, $0\le q<Q$; equivalently
$p=\lfloor x/Q\rfloor$, $q=x\bmod Q$. The \emph{reshape} of
$|\psi\rangle=\sum_x\psi(x)|x\rangle$ across the cut is
$\Psi\in\mathbb{C}^{P\times Q}$ with $\Psi[p,q]=\psi(pQ+q)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Bond dimension]
A matrix $M\in\mathbb{C}^{P\times Q}$ \emph{has bond} $D$ if $M=AB$ for some
$A\in\mathbb{C}^{P\times D}$, $B\in\mathbb{C}^{D\times Q}$. The \emph{minimal bond
dimension} of $M$ is the least such $D$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Bond dimension equals rank]\label{prop:bond}
$M$ has bond $D$ if and only if $D\ge\rank M$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $M=AB$ with inner index of size $D$ then $\rank M\le\rank A\le D$. Conversely
a rank factorisation attains $D=\rank M$, and any larger $D$ is obtained by
padding both cores with zeros; the padded terms contribute zero to every entry.
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:bond} is why the paper is a computation of ranks: the
Schmidt rank across a cut \emph{is} the minimal bond dimension there, so a rank
lower bound is an unconditional lower bound on the memory and time of any
tensor-train method that materialises the state.

\begin{definition}[Periodic comb]
For $r\ge1$ and offset $x_0$, the \emph{comb of period $r$} in a register of
dimension $n=PQ$ is
$|\mathrm{comb}\rangle=\sum_{0\le x<n,\ x\equiv x_0\,(r)}|x\rangle$, whose
reshape is the $0/1$ matrix
\[
M[p,q]=\begin{cases}1,&(pQ+q)\bmod r=x_0\bmod r,\\0,&\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

The reshape is faithful because $\lfloor x/Q\rfloor\cdot Q+(x\bmod Q)=x$.

\begin{definition}[Reduced period]
For $r,Q\ge1$ set $g=\gcd(r,Q)$ and $s(r,Q)=r/g$, the order of the cyclic
subgroup $\langle Q\rangle\le\mathbb{Z}/r\mathbb{Z}$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Why the comb is the state that matters}

Quantum order finding prepares $n^{-1/2}\sum_{x<n}|x\rangle|y^x\bmod N\rangle$ on
a register of dimension $n=2^L$, measures the second register, and collapses the
first onto $\sum_{x<n,\ x\equiv x_0\,(r)}|x\rangle$ with $r=\ord_N(y)$. The
Fourier transform is applied to \emph{this} state; everything downstream is a
consequence of its transform. Hence the compressibility of order finding by
tensor trains is exactly the compressibility of the comb.

\section{Two elementary rank tools}

\begin{lemma}[Identity submatrix bound]\label{lem:delta}
Let $A\in\mathbb{C}^{P\times Q}$ and let $f:\{0,\dots,k-1\}\to\{0,\dots,P-1\}$,
$g:\{0,\dots,k-1\}\to\{0,\dots,Q-1\}$ be arbitrary maps with
$A[f(i),g(j)]=\delta_{ij}$. Then $\rank A\ge k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $E[i,p]=[p=f(i)]$ and $F[q,j]=[q=g(j)]$. Then $(EA)[i,q]=A[f(i),q]$ and
$(XF)[i,j]=X[i,g(j)]$, so $E(AF)=I_k$; rank is submultiplicative, whence
$k\le\rank(AF)\le\rank A$. Injectivity of $f,g$ is not assumed; it is forced by
the delta condition.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Diagonal submatrix bound]\label{lem:diag}
The same conclusion holds if merely $A[f(i),g(i)]\neq0$ for all $i$ and
$A[f(i),g(j)]=0$ for $i\neq j$: rescale the rows of $E$ by $A[f(i),g(i)]^{-1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Phases are free]\label{lem:phase}
If $d\in(\mathbb{C}^\times)^P$ and $e\in(\mathbb{C}^\times)^Q$ then
$\rank\big(\mathrm{diag}(d)X\,\mathrm{diag}(e)\big)=\rank X$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Submultiplicativity gives $\le$; conjugating back by $\mathrm{diag}(d^{-1})$ and
$\mathrm{diag}(e^{-1})$ recovers $X$ and gives $\ge$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:phase} has algorithmic meaning: the local single-qubit phase gates
that a tensor-train emulation of the Fourier transform applies to individual
cores cannot change any bond dimension.

\section{The exact bond dimension of a comb}

\subsection{Upper bound: an explicit two-core tensor train}

The left block communicates with the right block only through the residue
$pQ\bmod r$, and every such residue is a multiple of $g=\gcd(r,Q)$, so the
traffic across the cut is confined to a set of size $s=r/g$.

\begin{definition}[Reduced cores]\label{def:cores}
With $s=s(r,Q)$ and $g=\gcd(r,Q)$, set
\[
A[p,c]=\big[\,pQ\bmod r=cg\,\big],\qquad
B[c,q]=\big[\,(cg+q)\bmod r=x_0\bmod r\,\big],\qquad c<s.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact factorisation]\label{thm:factor}
For all $P,Q,x_0$ and $r\ge1$ we have $M=AB$; hence $\rank M\le s(r,Q)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fix $p$. Since $g\mid pQ\bmod r$ there is exactly one $c<s$ with $pQ\bmod r=cg$,
so $\sum_cA[p,c]B[c,q]$ has a single surviving term, equal to
$[(pQ\bmod r+q)\bmod r=x_0\bmod r]=M[p,q]$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Lower bound: an explicit delta submatrix}

\begin{lemma}[Arithmetic heart]\label{lem:heart}
Let $r\ge1$, $k=\min(P,s(r,Q))$, and for $i,j<k$ put $f(i)=i$ and
$g_{\mathrm{col}}(j)=\big(x_0+(r-(jQ\bmod r))\big)\bmod r$. If $r\le Q$, so that
$g_{\mathrm{col}}(j)<Q$ is a legal column index, then
$f(i)Q+g_{\mathrm{col}}(j)\equiv x_0\pmod r$ if and only if $i=j$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Modulo $r$ the quantity equals $x_0+(i-j)Q$, so the congruence holds iff
$s\mid i-j$, and $|i-j|<k\le s$ forces $i=j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact rank of the comb]\label{thm:main}
For $0<r\le Q$ and any $P,x_0$,
\[
\rank M=\min\!\left(P,\ \frac{r}{\gcd(r,Q)}\right).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The upper bound is Theorem~\ref{thm:factor} together with $\rank M\le P$. The
lower bound is Lemma~\ref{lem:delta} applied to the index maps of
Lemma~\ref{lem:heart}.
\end{proof}

Neither term of the minimum is decorative: $P=4$, $Q=16$, $r=5$ gives rank
$4=\min(4,5)$. Nor is $r\le Q$: with $Q$ smaller than the period some residue
classes are empty in the right block and the count of nonzero rows drops.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:coprime}
If $\gcd(Q,r)=1$ and $r\le P$, $r\le Q$, then $\rank M=r$ exactly; in particular
the comb is a product state across the cut iff $r=1$.
\end{corollary}

At the opposite extreme, if $r\mid Q$ then all rows coincide and $\rank M\le1$:
the comb is unentangled across that cut however large $r$ is. Theorem~\ref{thm:main}
interpolates between these two extremes ($g=1$ and $s=1$).

\begin{theorem}[Operational form]\label{thm:oper}
For $0<r\le Q$, a tensor train of the comb across the cut with bond index of size
$D$ exists if and only if $D\ge\min(P,r/\gcd(r,Q))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:main} with Proposition~\ref{prop:bond}; constructively,
use the cores of Definition~\ref{def:cores} when $s\le P$ and $M=I_PM$ when
$P\le s$.
\end{proof}

\section{Binary registers: the odd-part law}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:gcd2}
If $m$ is odd and $t\le b$ then $\gcd(2^tm,2^b)=2^t$ and hence $s(2^tm,2^b)=m$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Odd-part law]\label{thm:oddpart}
Across the binary cut $2^a\otimes2^b$, a comb of period $r=2^tm$ with $m$ odd,
$t\le b$ and $r\le2^b$ has
\[
\rank M=\min(2^a,m).
\]
The power-of-two part of the period contributes nothing; the odd part
contributes everything.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Product-state dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:oddpart} with $a\ge1$, the comb is a
product state across the cut if and only if $m=1$, i.e.\ iff the period is a pure
power of two.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp de-quantization barrier]\label{thm:barrier}
Every tensor-train / matrix-product-state representation of the comb across the
binary cut has bond dimension $D\ge\min(2^a,m)$, with $m$ the odd part of the
period.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential barrier at the balanced cut]\label{thm:exponential}
On $L=2a$ qubits split down the middle ($P=Q=2^a$), the comb of period
$r=2^a-1$ requires bond dimension $D\ge2^a-1=2^{L/2}-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Consecutive integers are coprime, so $\gcd(2^a-1,2^a)=1$ and the reduced period
is $2^a-1$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:main} and Proposition~\ref{prop:bond}.
\end{proof}

The period $2^a-1$ is not adversarial exotica: it is the magnitude of order one
expects for a generic base modulo a generic modulus, and being coprime to the
block size it is the worst case for compressibility.

\begin{example}
For $P=Q=8$, $x_0=0$, the ranks for $r=1,\dots,8$ are $1,1,3,1,5,3,7,1$ ---
exactly the odd parts of $1,\dots,8$. An exhaustive check over all
$1\le P,Q,r\le12$, $0\le x_0\le3$ (6912 matrices) finds the rank always equal to
the number of distinct nonzero rows and, whenever $r\le Q$, to
$\min(P,r/\gcd(r,Q))$, with zero mismatches.
\end{example}

\section{From values to support}

\begin{definition}
$N\in\mathbb{C}^{P\times Q}$ is \emph{supported on the comb} $(r,x_0)$ if for all
$p,q$: $N[p,q]\neq0\iff(pQ+q)\bmod r=x_0\bmod r$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Support-only lower bound]\label{thm:support}
If $0<r\le Q$ and $N$ is supported on the comb $(r,x_0)$, then
$\rank N\ge\min(P,r/\gcd(r,Q))$, whatever its nonzero values are.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Use the index maps of Lemma~\ref{lem:heart}: on the diagonal the support
condition holds, off the diagonal it fails, so Lemma~\ref{lem:diag} applies.
\end{proof}

\section{The Fourier transform of an exact comb}

Assume here the divisible case $n=mr$, so the comb $\sum_{j<m}|x_0+jr\rangle$ has
exactly $m$ teeth and wraps the register perfectly.

\begin{theorem}[Sharp-peak theorem]\label{thm:peak}
With $\zeta=e^{2\pi i/n}$ and $n=mr$,
\[
\Psi(k)=\sum_{j<m}\zeta^{(x_0+jr)k}
=\begin{cases}m\,\zeta^{x_0k},&m\mid k,\\0,&\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Factor out $\zeta^{x_0k}$ and sum the geometric series
$\sum_{j<m}(\zeta^{rk})^j$; since $\zeta^r=e^{2\pi i/m}$, the ratio is an $m$-th
root of unity, equal to $1$ exactly when $m\mid k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\Psi(k)\neq0\iff m\mid k$: the transform of a comb of period $r$ is a comb of
period $m=n/r$, decorated with phases.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Transform rank law]\label{thm:transform}
Reshape the transformed state as $\Psi[p,q]=\Psi(pQ+q)$. If $m\le Q$ then
$\rank\Psi=\min\!\big(P,\ m/\gcd(m,Q)\big)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:peak}, $\Psi=\mathrm{diag}(d)M_m\,\mathrm{diag}(e)$ where
$M_m$ is the $0/1$ comb matrix of period $m$ and offset $0$, $d_p=m\zeta^{x_0pQ}$
and $e_q=\zeta^{x_0q}$ are nonzero. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:phase} and
Theorem~\ref{thm:main}. A second, independent proof of the lower bound comes from
Theorem~\ref{thm:support}, and it is that argument which survives into the
approximate setting.
\end{proof}

Thus the Fourier transform does not preserve bond dimension: it \emph{inverts}
the period, $r\mapsto m=n/r$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:gcdprod}
If $Q>0$ and $Q\mid rm$ then $Q\le\gcd(r,Q)\gcd(m,Q)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\gcd(Q,rm)\mid\gcd(Q,r)\gcd(Q,m)$ and $\gcd(Q,rm)=Q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Complementarity]\label{thm:compl}
Let $n=rm=PQ$ with $Q>0$, $r\le Q$, $m\le Q$. Then
\[
\rank(M)\cdot\rank(\Psi)\ \le\ P=\frac{n}{Q}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The two ranks are at most $s(r,Q)$ and $s(m,Q)$, and
$\frac{r}{\gcd(r,Q)}\cdot\frac{m}{\gcd(m,Q)}=\frac{rm}{\gcd(r,Q)\gcd(m,Q)}
\le\frac{rm}{Q}=P$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:gcdprod}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
At a balanced cut $P=Q=\sqrt n$, at least one of the two bond dimensions is at
most $n^{1/4}$: an exact comb cannot be tensor-train-hard both in position space
and in frequency space.
\end{corollary}

\begin{example}
$n=12=3\cdot4$, cut $P=3$, $Q=4$, input period $r=3$, co-period $m=4$: the input
rank is $\min(3,3/\gcd(3,4))=3$, the output rank is $\min(3,4/\gcd(4,4))=1$, and
the product $3=P$ attains the bound.
\end{example}

\section{Where de-quantization stops: the divisibility boundary}

Theorem~\ref{thm:compl} requires $r\mid n$. Quantum order finding runs on a
binary register, $n=2^L$, and the order $r=\ord_N(y)$ is generically odd, or at
least has a large odd part; an odd $r>1$ never divides $2^L$. The realised
post-measurement state is therefore the \emph{truncated} comb
$\{x<n:x\equiv x_0\ (r)\}$ analysed in Sections~4--5. Consequently its bond
dimension is $\min(2^a,m)$ with $m$ the odd part of $r$ --- exponential in $L$ at
any reasonably balanced cut --- and, since a tensor-train sweep costs
$\Omega(D^2)$ per site, emulating the Fourier transform on it costs
$\Omega(m^2)$ per site.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
 & $r\mid n$ (exact comb) & $r\nmid n$ (truncated, the real case)\\
\midrule
input bond dimension & $\min(P,r/\gcd(r,Q))$ & $\min(2^a,\text{odd part of }r)$\\
output bond dimension & $\min(P,m/\gcd(m,Q))$ & $\min(P,J)$, $J$ = tooth count\\
product of the two & $\le n/Q$ & no such bound\\
classical emulation & efficient on the cheap side & $\Omega(m^2)$ per site\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

There is a structural irony: the reason order finding needs continued-fraction
post-processing is exactly that $r\nmid n$, so the imperfection that complicates
the algorithm is the same one that immunises it against tensor-train emulation.

It bears emphasis what is \emph{not} claimed. Nothing here shows that factoring
is classically hard, nor that some de-quantization avoiding the comb entirely is
impossible. What is established is that the specific and much-discussed route ---
represent the post-measurement state as a low-rank tensor train and sweep the
Fourier transform through it --- is closed by an exact formula rather than by an
asymptotic hunch.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A (exact bond dimension).} Input $P,Q,r,x_0$ with $0<r\le
Q$. Compute $g\leftarrow\gcd(r,Q)$, $s\leftarrow r/g$, return $\min(P,s)$. Cost:
one Euclidean algorithm, $O(\log r)$ bit operations, replacing an
$O(PQ\min(P,Q))$ Gaussian elimination. Correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.

\paragraph{Algorithm B (exact rank-$s$ tensor train).} For each $p$ set
$c\leftarrow(pQ\bmod r)/g$ and $A[p,c]\leftarrow1$; for each $c,q$ set
$B[c,q]\leftarrow[(cg+q)\bmod r=x_0\bmod r]$. Cost $O(Ps+sQ)$; the bond $s$ is
optimal whenever $s\le P$.

\paragraph{Algorithm C (tensor-train Fourier emulation with a cost
certificate).} Alternately contract cores with local phase operators and
re-truncate by singular value decomposition, at cost $O(LD^3)$. Algorithm A
certifies the required $D$ beforehand: for a comb input $D=\min(P,s(r,Q))$, so
the emulation is efficient exactly when the reduced period is small, and is
refused rather than attempted when the odd part of the order is large.

\section{Open problems}

\subsection{The transformed truncated comb: a corrected law}

One naturally conjectures a \emph{Dirichlet smearing} principle: that for
 $n=2^L$ and odd $r\nmid n$ the transformed truncated comb
 $\Psi(k)=\sum_{x<n,\ x\equiv x_0\,(r)}e^{2\pi ixk/n}$ never vanishes, so that
 Theorem~\ref{thm:support} immediately yields maximal Schmidt rank. That
 statement is \emph{false}; the correct statement is an exact count.

\begin{proposition}[Zero-count law]\label{prop:zerocount}
Let $n=2^L$, let $r$ be odd, let $x_0<r$, and let
$J=\#\{x<n:x\equiv x_0\ (\mathrm{mod}\ r)\}$ be the number of teeth. Then
$\Psi(k)=0$ for exactly $\gcd(n,J)-1$ values of $k\in\{0,\dots,n-1\}$, namely the
nonzero multiples of $n/\gcd(n,J)$. In particular the spectrum has full support
if and only if $J$ is odd.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
With $\omega=e^{2\pi i/n}$ and $x_j=x_0+jr$ we have
$\Psi(k)=\omega^{x_0k}\sum_{j<J}(\omega^{rk})^j$. Since $r$ is odd and $n$ is a
power of two, $\gcd(r,n)=1$, so $\omega^{rk}=1$ iff $k=0$, and $\Psi(0)=J\neq0$.
For $k\neq0$ the geometric sum equals $(1-\omega^{rkJ})/(1-\omega^{rk})$, which
vanishes iff $n\mid rkJ$, iff $n\mid kJ$, iff $(n/\gcd(n,J))\mid k$; there are
$\gcd(n,J)-1$ such $k$ in $\{1,\dots,n-1\}$.
\end{proof}

The smallest counterexample to the naive guess is $n=16$, $r=5$, $x_0=0$, where
$J=4$ and the transform vanishes at $k=4,8,12$. Exhaustive computation for
$n=2^L$, $2\le L\le9$, odd $r\le11$, $x_0\in\{0,1\}$ confirms the count
$\gcd(n,J)-1$ without exception.

The zeros are a vanishing fraction of the spectrum and the intended conclusion
survives them. Expanding
$\Psi[p,q]=\sum_{j<J}\omega^{x_jpQ}\cdot\omega^{x_jq}$ exhibits the reshaped
transform as a product $AB$ with $A[p,j]=\omega^{x_jpQ}$ of size $P\times J$ and
$B[j,q]=\omega^{x_jq}$ of size $J\times Q$.

\begin{proposition}[Tooth bound]\label{prop:tooth}
$\rank\Psi\le\min(P,Q,J)$ always, and $\rank\Psi=J$ exactly when
$J\le\min(P,Q)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The inequality is the factorisation above. For equality, $B$ is Vandermonde in
the distinct nodes $\omega^{x_j}$, and $A^{\mathsf T}$ is Vandermonde in the nodes
$\omega^{x_jQ}$, distinct because $r$ is odd and $P$ is a power of two, so the
$x_j$ are distinct modulo $P$ for $j<J\le P$. Both factors have rank $J$, and
Sylvester's rank inequality gives $\rank(AB)\ge J$.
\end{proof}

\begin{conjecture}[Saturation for many teeth]
With $n=2^L$, $r$ odd, and a cut $n=PQ$ with $P\le Q$: if $J\ge P$ then
$\rank\Psi=P$, i.e.\ the transformed truncated comb has maximal Schmidt rank.
\end{conjecture}

\noindent\emph{Evidence and heuristic.} Cauchy--Binet expands each $P\times P$
minor of $AB$ as a sum, over $P$-subsets of the teeth, of products of two
nonzero Vandermonde determinants; the conjecture is that these do not conspire to
cancel. Exact computation in a finite field containing a genuine element of
order $n$ --- so that no floating-point tolerance is involved --- gives
$\rank\Psi=\min(P,J)$ in every case tested for $n=16,64,256$, odd $r\le13$,
$x_0\in\{0,1\}$. Proving it would make the tensor-train emulation maximally
expensive on \emph{both} sides of the transform, closing the escape route left
open by Theorem~\ref{thm:compl}. Floating-point rank estimation is misleading
here: for small $r$ the transformed comb has rapidly decaying singular values and
appears rank-deficient at tolerance $10^{-9}$ while being exactly of full rank ---
which is precisely why the robustness question below is the substantive one.

\subsection{Robustness}

\begin{conjecture}[No $\varepsilon$-de-quantization]
Let $M$ be the comb matrix with $r\le Q$ and $K=\min(P,r/\gcd(r,Q))$. Then for
every matrix $B$ of rank at most $D<K$,
\[
\|M-B\|_F^2\ \ge\ (K-D)\cdot\min_c(\mu_c\nu_c),
\]
where $\mu_c$ and $\nu_c$ count the left and right indices in residue class $c$.
In particular a matrix product state of bond dimension $D=\mathrm{poly}(L)$
cannot approximate the qubit comb to relative Frobenius error $o(1)$ when the odd
part of $r$ is exponential.
\end{conjecture}

\noindent\emph{Heuristic.} The comb has an explicit singular value
decomposition: it is a sum of $K$ rank-one terms whose left factors are pairwise
orthogonal indicators and whose right factors are likewise pairwise orthogonal
indicators, so its singular values are exactly $\sigma_c=\sqrt{\mu_c\nu_c}$. The
Eckart--Young theorem converts the exact rank statement into the stated
approximation bound.

\section{Discussion and future work}

Template~1 (bounded entanglement) is confirmed as the right frame and then turned
against the quantum-advantage-sceptical reading: the comb's entanglement entropy
across a balanced cut is $\log_2\min(2^a,m)$, linear in $L$ for a generic order,
so the hypothesis fails. Template~3 (tensor-train Fourier emulation) is proved
correct in the exact regime --- Theorem~\ref{thm:compl} gives it a quantitative
form stronger than usually stated --- while being inapplicable in the regime
order finding occupies. Template~2 concerns a different structural hypothesis and
is untouched.

Every hypothesis in the main theorem is used: dropping $r\le Q$ breaks the lower
bound; dropping the $P$ from the minimum breaks the formula; dropping
coprimality in Corollary~\ref{cor:coprime} collapses the rank to $1$ when
$r\mid Q$. The formula $\min(P,r/\gcd(r,Q))$ is the unique statement consistent
with all boundary cases.

Beyond the two conjectures: extend the computation to multi-cut matrix product
states, where one expects the odd-part law cutwise; analyse noisy combs using the
explicit singular value decomposition as unperturbed reference; identify other
quantum subroutines whose input compressibility is controlled by a purely
arithmetic invariant; and quantify how close a period must be to a power of two
before the comb becomes approximately compressible.

\section{Summary of results}

\begin{enumerate}
\item A tensor train across a cut with inner index $D$ exists iff
$D\ge\rank$ of the reshaped amplitude matrix.
\item For $0<r\le Q$, $\rank M=\min(P,r/\gcd(r,Q))$, independent of the offset.
\item Across a binary cut the rank is $\min(2^a,m)$ with $m$ the odd part of the
period; the comb is a product state iff the period is a power of two.
\item On $L=2a$ qubits at the balanced cut, period $2^a-1$ forces bond dimension
$\ge2^{L/2}-1$.
\item Any matrix supported exactly on a comb pattern has rank at least
$\min(P,r/\gcd(r,Q))$.
\item Invertible diagonal rescalings preserve rank: local phase gates cannot
alter bond dimension.
\item For an exact comb in a register of size $n=mr$, the transformed state has
rank $\min(P,m/\gcd(m,Q))$: the period is inverted.
\item In the exact case the product of input and output bond dimensions is at
most $n/Q$; at a balanced cut one side is always $\le n^{1/4}$.
\item All efficiency statements require $r\mid n$, which a binary register with
an order of large odd part violates; there the tensor-train route is sealed at
$\Omega(m^2)$ per site.
\end{enumerate}

